### 7. Did bacterial pneumonia occur as a common complication of influenza in the 1918 pandemic regardless of whether patients wore masks?
Answer: Yes, bacterial pneumonia was a common and often fatal complication of influenza during the 1918 pandemic, occurring as a secondary infection after the virus damaged the respiratory tract, regardless of mask use. The NIH report states that "most victims succumbed to bacterial pneumonia following influenza virus infection" and that "the pneumonia was caused when bacteria that normally inhabit the nose and throat invaded the lungs along a pathway created when the virus destroyed the cells that line the bronchial tubes and lungs." The source makes no mention of face masks as a cause of bacterial pneumonia.

Source URL: https://www.nih.gov/news-events/news-releases/bacterial-pneumonia-caused-most-deaths-1918-influenza-pandemic

### 9. How do historians and epidemiologists currently explain the high death toll from bacterial pneumonia in the 1918 pandemic?
Answer: Historians and epidemiologists currently explain the high death toll from bacterial pneumonia in the 1918 pandemic by concluding that "the majority of deaths … likely resulted directly from secondary bacterial pneumonia caused by common upper respiratory-tract bacteria" (Morens et al., *The Journal of Infectious Diseases*, 2008). The influenza virus damaged the respiratory epithelium, enabling aggressive bacterial invasion, and without such secondary infections most patients would have recovered.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/198/7/962/2192118

